; Start of the game, restart point and main loop
;
; The entry point of the game: the BASIC loader ends with PRINT USR 27648. This
; entry also holds the restart point and the main loop. How the whole game
; runs, top down:
; .
; Start. The four state blocks that SAVE writes (the variables at $B6EB, the
; objects and characters at $C11B, the timers at $CA84, the locations at
; $BA8A) hold the story as loaded from tape. The game copies them to a
; backup: the big two to StateBackup ($F400), the small two to $5F00. The
; title screen (the loading picture) is still on the display.
; .
; Restart (RestartGame, $6C27). QUIT, death and a tape error all jump here.
; The stack is reset, the backup is copied back over the state blocks (so the
; story begins again), the border goes black and the game waits at the title
; screen for any key. If N is held when the key goes down the pictures are
; turned off for the whole game. The Z80 refresh register read at that moment
; is the seed of the random numbers.
; .
; Set up. The cursors and column counters of the story window and the input
; window get their start values, the order slots (what characters were told to
; do) are cleared, the printer is off, messages are on, the score is 0, the
; screen is cleared and a rule is drawn between the story and the input
; window. The first 17 lines of story text will scroll without the pause that
; otherwise follows each full screen.
; .
; Opening. The first time round (ParserWordCount is still $FF from the saved
; state) the game sets up its hidden starting state (InitNewGame: which exit
; is blocked, the random event, who is about), echoes "> LOOK" in the input
; window and runs that command as if it had been typed, so the player starts
; with the description and the picture of Bag End.
; .
; Main loop (MainLoop, $6D13), once per typed line. ReadLine reads a line of
; up to 128 characters from the keyboard into InputLine. ReadTypedWord turns
; its words into tokens in ParsedWords: a word of the dictionary, THEN (a full
; stop), AND (a comma), a double quote or the end of the line. A word that is
; not in the dictionary prints `I do not know the word "..."` and drops the
; line. What is typed between double quotes (SAY TO somebody "...") is an order
; for a character: the closing quote gets a THEN before it, and a line that
; ends inside quotes is refused. ParseLine then builds phrase records from the
; tokens, one sentence (up to a THEN) at a time, and RunSentences runs them: it
; matches each record to a command, finds the objects, checks the command and,
; if it is allowed, performs it and gives the others their turn. The loop goes
; round ParseLine and RunSentences while ParserFlag stays set, that is while
; the line has sentences left, and then reads the next line. A sentence that
; ParseLine cannot understand (it prints "What ?") drops the rest of the line.
; The key @ at the start of an empty line repeats the previous line: the
; editor returns Z and the loop goes straight to RunWordList with the old word
; list.
; .
; A turn. After each sentence the game performed, #R$7960 ticks the timers
; and runs every other character (one turn of the world) before the next
; sentence of the line is run. A sentence the game refused takes no turn.
; .
; Hardware: DI (interrupts stay off for the whole game); OUT to port $FE for
; the border and BORDCR for the border colour the ROM would use; IN from port
; $FE for the title-screen key (all half-rows) and the N key (half-row $7F);
; the R register as the random seed; the screen bitmap and attributes (cleared
; with ClearScreen, the rule drawn from BorderTile). All other output goes
; through the print routines.
@label=GameStart
c$6C00 DI            ; interrupts stay off for the whole game
 $6C01 LD DE,$F400   ; back up the objects and characters ...
 $6C04 LD HL,$C11B   ;
@isub=LD BC,STATE_OBJECTS_SIZE
 $6C07 LD BC,$0615   ;
 $6C0A LDIR          ;
 $6C0C LD HL,$BA8A   ; ... and the locations (DE goes on after the first copy)
@isub=LD BC,STATE_LOCATIONS_SIZE
 $6C0F LD BC,$05D9   ;
 $6C12 LDIR          ;
 $6C14 LD DE,$5F00   ; back up the saved variables ...
 $6C17 LD HL,$B6EB   ;
@isub=LD BC,STATE_VARS_SIZE
 $6C1A LD BC,$001D   ;
 $6C1D LDIR          ;
 $6C1F LD HL,$CA84   ; ... and the timers and character records
@isub=LD BC,STATE_TIMERS_SIZE
 $6C22 LD BC,$00BF   ;
 $6C25 LDIR          ;
